    "text": "To construct the circumcircle of a triangle, which geometric lines are constructed to locate the circumcentre?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Medians from the vertices",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Lines parallel to the opposite sides",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Perpendicular bisectors of the sides of the triangle",
        "isCorrect": true
      },
      {
        "text": "Angle bisectors of the angles",
        "isCorrect": false
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "C",
    "solution": "Step 1: The circumcircle passes through all three vertices of the triangle.\nStep 2: Its centre (circumcentre) is equidistant from all three vertices.\nStep 3: By the perpendicular bisector theorem, the circumcentre is the point of concurrency of the perpendicular bisectors of the sides.",
